#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176807} :activity/id #uuid "eb431efc-453b-5866-adc7-559d3c697e3e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050332 :topic/id #uuid "40868547-c38a-58e8-b266-21013ff254f9" :topic/math-academy-id 3627} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175838 :question/id #uuid "11d9077c-a0ac-5d42-9616-b8fe867d1db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9076"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416112}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "efebb741-9ea8-56f3-9f79-7c340d1fe301" :step/math-academy-id 33843 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176806}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176023 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175878 :question/id #uuid "1ee74b2f-adff-5c38-8b68-753b1c02f91c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9550"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2eca638-7c7b-51ed-99e3-3b3156d13669" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416110}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "736cedc4-b5c1-5e97-b975-c6db4fd154c1" :step/math-academy-id 35775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177037 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\left( e^{-x^2}- x^2y \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left(y+xy^2\\right) \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the unit circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1,$ oriented counterclockwise. \n\nDirect evaluation of this integral is likely to be a difficult task. However, we can make light work of this tricky integral using Green's theorem.\n\nRecall that Green's theorem states the following:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ (shown below) satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/dd/dd859c753a8a5445eafab5db4f1e796b48997e1abb1728accd03fe3825e595d6.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^{-x^2}- x^2y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe obtain the partial derivatives   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{-{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis double integral will be easier to evaluate if we switch to plane polar coordinates. \n\nThe region $D$ in $xy$-coordinates can be represented as the region $\\Delta$ in polar coordinates, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral by applying the change of variables, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0b5a23c7-4ccf-5122-8b5f-98fbabb0e367" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5838 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0b3de726-ead0-5369-a1fe-3dcc61b38bbc" :step/math-academy-id 35776 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176808}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175879 :question/id #uuid "40a301b7-9eaa-59e1-bcb0-4a35fecbff69" :question/math-academy-id "e-9551"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416111}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "f69ccccf-5d34-5f08-95d0-7af5d0c7628b" :step/math-academy-id 35777 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176735}}] :activity/title "Green's Theorem in Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :